PLACES=[
N('angell-house-leavenworth-ks','A. J. Angell House','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.31188,-94.92142,'exact','view','drive','','1883–1885',
  'A. J. Angell came to Leavenworth from New York in 1857, worked 13 years as a surveyor and in 1870 started a lumber business that became one of the town\'s most successful. In August 1883 he was given a building permit for a brick and frame house on South Broadway, costing $15,000. He died in June 1885. The house stood vacant from 1913 to 1918 and was vandalised, then stayed with the Webster family, descendants of his daughter, from 1921 until 1975.',
  'A large two-and-a-half-storey brick house with a basement, built in the Queen Anne style. The 1977 form describes dressed-stone bands joining the window sills and lintels, a wide cornice on closely spaced wooden brackets, a hipped roof broken by five brick chimneys, and inside an oak breakfront and wainscot in the dining room with a walnut and maple parquet floor. A carriage house also stands. The form could not name the architect or builder. Inside not checked since 1977.',
  'A Queen Anne lumberman\'s house with five brick chimneys.',
  'At 714 South Broadway, a private house as far as the 1977 form shows; who owns it now and whether it is open were not checked. Look from the public sidewalk.',
  [['National Register form, A. J. Angell House (77000586)',NR%'77000586'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR]],brick='yes'),
N('insley-house-leavenworth-ks','Merritt Insley House and outbuildings','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.31971,-94.91758,'exact','view','drive','','1860–1883',
  'The two-storey brick rear wing of this house was finished in 1860. In 1865 and 1866 an Italianate brick cube was added to its south wall, which turned the main front from east to south. In 1883 a five-sided bay was added to the west wall. A carriage house (about 1873), a brick outhouse, a cast-iron fence and old brick sidewalks complete the property. The 1986 form found few alterations.',
  'A two-storey brick Italianate house on a coursed limestone foundation with a cut limestone water table, at Fifth and Seneca streets. The cast-iron fence and brick sidewalks run along its east and south sides, and a one-storey brick outhouse stands to the north. Inside not checked.',
  'A brick Italianate house with its iron fence, carriage house and privy still in place.',
  'At 602 Seneca Street. The 1986 form lists it as a private residence; who owns it now was not checked. Look from the public sidewalk.',
  [['National Register form, Merritt Insley House (86002801)',NR%'86002801'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR3]],brick='yes'),
N('burt-house-leavenworth-ks','Nathaniel H. Burt House','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.30622,-94.91792,'exact','view','drive','','1895',
  'Nathaniel Burt, a prominent Leavenworth businessman, built this house in 1895 for his wife and two sons. William Pratt Feth, a Leavenworth architect just back from study in Chicago, designed it in a Romanesque Revival style that Wikipedia likens to the work of H. H. Richardson. Burt\'s sons were involved in the Great Western Stove Company. The house passed through several families after 1962 and was refurbished inside in the early 1980s.',
  'An irregular brick house with limestone trim and foundation, highly ornamented, about 54 by 65 feet, with a matching carriage house of 1920 behind it. Wikipedia describes a large stained glass window on the stairway, oak, cherry and bird\'s-eye maple pocket doors and a foyer chandelier, but a private house is not open, so this is not seen from the street.',
  'A Romanesque Revival house by a Leavenworth architect with some 60 buildings to his name.',
  'Wikipedia gives the address as both 400 and 1200 Fifth Avenue; the Kansas Historical Society says 1200 Fifth Avenue, and the pin is on that house, next to the Carroll Mansion museum. It looks like a private house: look from the sidewalk.',
  [['National Register form, Nathaniel H. Burt House (87001105)',NR%'87001105'],W('Nathaniel H. Burt House'),['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR2]],brick='?'),
N('william-small-home-leavenworth-ks','William Small Memorial Home for Aged Women','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.32372,-94.92335,'exact','view','drive','','1903–1974',
  'Zephy Small, widow of the Leavenworth businessman William Small, paid for this brick home, built in 1903 and 1904, as a purpose-built house for the Old Ladies\' Rest, a charity that offered women over sixty an alternative to the county poorhouse. It was the third building the charity used and is the only one still standing. It served as the Small Home until 1974. William Feth was the architect.',
  'A Classical Revival brick building on North Broadway. The 2020 Register summary does not say what has happened to the inside, or what the building is used for now.',
  'A charity home for elderly women, built by a widow in 1904.',
  'At 711 North Broadway. A Wikimedia Commons caption for a 2024 photograph says it is now the Landmark Apartments, so it is private: look from the sidewalk. Its walls are buff brick, seen in that photograph.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings (William Small Memorial Home, listed 2020)',KSR6]],brick='no'),
N('north-broadway-school-leavenworth-ks','North Broadway School','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.32468,-94.92321,'exact','view','drive','','1923–1999',
  'The Leavenworth school district hired architect Charles A. Smith to design four new schools in the 1920s, among them this one and Third Avenue School; the two have nearly identical plans. Gordon Walker Construction Company of Salina built them. North Broadway opened on September 17, 1923 with 382 pupils, was an elementary school until 1999, held other educational programmes until 2010, and was then sold.',
  'A two-storey U-shaped building that fills a whole block: a concrete frame faced with tapestry brick and cast stone, with pointed-arch cast-stone doorways at the front entrances and diamond patterns in the brickwork, in Tudor Revival style. The Register form lists it as vacant. Inside not checked.',
  'A Tudor Revival school block of 1923 with diamond-patterned brick.',
  'At 801 North Broadway. Sold in 2010; present owner and use were not checked. Look from the public sidewalk.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings (North Broadway School, listed 2011)',KSR4]],brick='yes'),
N('sumner-school-leavenworth-ks','Sumner Elementary School','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.30284,-94.91686,'exact','view','drive','','1925–1969',
  'Sumner was one of two elementary schools Leavenworth built for its Black pupils; Lincoln School served North Leavenworth and Sumner the south. The first Sumner School went up on this site in 1866. By 1915 it could not hold its 185 pupils, and Charles Ashley Smith designed the present two-storey brick building, finished in 1925 under the long-time principal and educator Blanch K. Bruce. It served South Leavenworth\'s African American families until the schools were desegregated after Brown v. Board, and closed after the 1968 to 1969 school year. The district then used it as a maintenance building, and in 2000 the Pentecostal Church of the Apostolic Faith bought it as an activity centre.',
  'A two-storey brick Commercial-style school in a neighbourhood of houses and churches. Inside not checked.',
  'A 1925 school built for Leavenworth\'s Black children.',
  'At 1501 Fifth Avenue. Owned by a church as of the 2011 listing; whether it is open was not checked. Look from the public sidewalk.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings (Sumner Elementary School, listed 2011)',KSR5]],brick='?'),
N('axa-building-leavenworth-ks','AXA Building (Eppenscheid Building)','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.31783,-94.91512,'exact','view','drive','','1905',
  'Built in 1905 at 205 South Fifth Street for Charles Eppenscheid, a St. Louis investor, and first known as the Eppenscheid Building. Wikipedia names the architect as William B. Feth and the builder as R. B. Yoakum; the Kansas Historical Society gives the architect as William P. Feth. It was listed on the National Register in 1972. Wikipedia calls the style Late Victorian and the Historical Society calls it Beaux Arts.',
  'An ornate two-and-a-half-storey red brick commercial building with a 125-foot front on Fifth Street. A guide to Kansas architecture, quoted by Wikipedia, says it stands out for its neo-classical ornament at the cornice, the second-floor bay windows and the lobby entrance. What is in it now was not checked.',
  'An ornate red brick commercial block of 1905 on Fifth Street.',
  'On South Fifth Street in the downtown district; look from the sidewalk. Present use and hours were not checked.',
  [W('AXA Building'),['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR]],brick='yes'),
N('helmers-manufacturing-building-leavenworth-ks','Helmers Manufacturing Company Building','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','industry','standing',39.29272,-94.90548,'site','view','drive','','1909–1940',
  'Built in 1909 for the Helmers furniture company, which began as a small barber-chair maker working from two downtown storefronts in the late 1880s and grew into a regional furniture maker with plants in Leavenworth and Kansas City. The company worked in this building until 1940. It was listed in 2009 as a good example of an early twentieth-century factory built between traditional and modern building methods.',
  'A plain factory with load-bearing brick walls and heavy timber framing, with steel lintels over grouped windows. The Register summary notes the lighting, heating and fire-safety systems that were up to date when it was built.',
  'A 1909 furniture works from the days when Leavenworth made chairs.',
  'At 300 Santa Fe Street (also given as 2500 South Second Street). Present owner and use were not checked. Look from the public street.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings (Helmers Manufacturing Company Building, listed 2009)',KSR2]],brick='yes'),
N('greenwood-cemetery-leavenworth-ks','Greenwood Cemetery','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','burial','standing',39.28857,-94.93976,'area','view','drive','','1863',
  'Mrs. Mary A. Davis, wife of the local physician Dr. James Davis, bought 12 acres of farmland in 1863 for this cemetery. The Pilot Knob and Mount Aurora burying ground, the first non-sectarian one near the town, had become hard to reach. The Register says Greenwood is a good example of the rural or garden cemetery design popular in the mid-nineteenth century, and calls it now abandoned.',
  'A rural cemetery on the edge of town laid out in the garden-cemetery style. How much of the layout and the stones can be seen was not checked.',
  'An abandoned garden cemetery founded in 1863, listed in 2022.',
  'Tonganoxie Road at Limit Street. The Register calls the cemetery abandoned, but who owns it and whether visitors may walk in were not checked, so view from the road.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings (Greenwood Cemetery, listed 2022)',KSR3]],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('giles-house-leavenworth-ks','Leanna Giles House','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.32309,-94.91188,'exact','view','drive','','ca. 1860',
  'Built about 1860, before Kansas became a state, this small vernacular house is one of the few pre-statehood "national folk" houses left in Leavenworth. It is named for Leanna Giles, who lived there longer than anyone, first as a tenant and then as owner. It was added to the Kansas Register of Historic Places in 2017.',
  'A modest vernacular house, once a common working-class type in the town. What it looks like now, and whether it is lived in, was not checked.',
  'One of Leavenworth\'s last houses from before statehood.',
  'At 202 Pottawatomie Street. A private house as far as the listing shows; look from the sidewalk.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings (Leanna Giles House, State Register 2017)',KSR3]],brick='?'),
N('richard-allen-cultural-center-leavenworth-ks','Richard Allen Cultural Center and Museum','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.32537,-94.91590,'exact','open','drive','','',
  'Opened in 1992 to tell the story of African Americans in Leavenworth, in the former home of Army Captain William Bly, a Buffalo Soldier in the First World War. The house is furnished as it would have looked in the early 1900s, and in 2002 an addition was built on the front for more displays. Wikipedia lists the Black Dignity photographs from the Mary Everhard collection, military items from African American soldiers who served at Fort Leavenworth, including Colin Powell, and a 2016 bust of Cathay Williams.',
  'A house museum with a modern front addition, with period rooms and displays of photographs and military objects. Wikipedia notes that the collection includes difficult material, such as a Ku Klux Klan costume and a newspaper on the lynching of Fred Alexander, kept so it is not forgotten.',
  'A museum of Black Leavenworth in a Buffalo Soldier\'s house.',
  'At 412 Kiowa Street, a short way from the gate of Fort Leavenworth. Its own page could not be read, so hours are not checked; call before going.',
  [W('Richard Allen Cultural Center')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('powers-house-leavenworth-ks','David Powers House','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.34995,-94.96266,'site','view','drive','','1872',
  'David Powers, a Kentucky-born freighter who moved to Kansas about 1856, made a fortune hauling freight across the plains and, when the railroad ended that trade about 1870, turned to cattle, becoming one of the state\'s leading cattlemen of the 1870s. He built this 17-room brick house in the Salt Creek valley northwest of Leavenworth in 1872 and lived there until he died in October 1884. His family sold it in 1906; the LaCaille family owned it from 1911 to 1946.',
  'An irregular two-storey brick Italianate house on a limestone foundation with a wide bracketed cornice, segmental arches on the first-floor windows and round-headed ones above. The porch over the front door has been removed. The form is from 1977; what it looks like now was not checked.',
  'The 17-room brick house of a cattle baron of the 1870s.',
  'The 1977 form calls it a private residence two miles northwest of Leavenworth and gives no street address. The pin is on the house seen on the aerial, about 40 metres from the Register list point. Do not enter private land; look from a public road if one passes it.',
  [['National Register form, David Powers House (77000588)',NR%'77000588']],brick='yes',conf='mixed'),
N('fort-sully-leavenworth-ks','Fort Sully earthworks, Fort Leavenworth','Fort Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','fort','traces',39.37,-94.93917,'area','permit','hike','','1864–1865',
  'Fort Sully was an earthwork artillery battery built in September and October 1864 on the plateau of Hancock Hill, the highest hill west of Fort Leavenworth, when Confederate General Sterling Price\'s raid into Missouri threatened the post. Heavy 24-pounder siege guns were hauled up from the fort\'s armoury. The threat ended with Price\'s defeat at Westport on October 23, 1864, and by June 1865 only four men were left there.',
  'Wikipedia says all that remains is the crater-like imprint of the earthwork, about 670 feet long on a plateau 25 to 100 feet wide, and that restricted access has kept it well preserved. Historical markers about it stand at the site, in the Fort Leavenworth National Cemetery below, and at the Frontier Army Museum, where two of the guns that were emplaced here are on show. Wikipedia describes a foot route from the powerline clearing behind Stanley Avenue and the cemetery, where a sign marks the Heritage Trail.',
  'Civil War gun pits on a hilltop inside Fort Leavenworth.',
  'It is inside the active Fort Leavenworth military post. A visitor without a Department of Defense card needs a pass from the post\'s Visitor Control Center at 1 Sherman Avenue; the post\'s gate page was quoted by a web search and could not be opened, so the current rules are not checked. Contact the post before going. The pin is the Wikipedia coordinate for the site and marks the area only: on the aerial it falls on a small round feature with a loop of road round it on the hilltop, and the long earthwork was not made out on the aerial or the LiDAR.',
  [W('Fort Sully (Fort Leavenworth)'),W('Fort Leavenworth'),['Fort Leavenworth gate information (Army)','https://home.army.mil/leavenworth/about/gate-information']],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('stonehaven-farm-tonganoxie-ks','Stonehaven Farm (Cora Wellhouse Bullard house)','Tonganoxie, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.13427,-95.05900,'site','view','drive','','1898–1903',
  'Stonehaven Farm is a 20-acre piece of a much larger orchard run by Frederick Wellhouse and his daughter Cora Wellhouse Bullard. The foundation of the house was begun in 1898, and the 6,000-square-foot Shingle style house was finished in 1903. Cora designed it and built part of it herself. Like her father she took part in county and state politics, farming and social causes. The Register lists it for her and for its architecture.',
  'A large Shingle style house with Craftsman touches, with farm buildings from the farm\'s years under Cora Bullard (1898 to 1926). What can be seen from the road was not checked.',
  'A shingle-style farmhouse designed and partly built by its owner, Cora Bullard.',
  'At 19801 Tonganoxie Drive, near Tonganoxie. It is a private farm: ask the owner or look from the public road. The pin comes from an address lookup and marks the site.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings (Stonehaven Farmstead, listed 2018)',KSR5]],brick='?'),
]
